<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    FS.GG.Kit receiver (ADR-0062, .github#1262). This repo is a `coordination-kit` receiver: it holds
    the shared coordination skills, the `fsgg-coord` client, and the engine tool manifest on disk. It
    used to take them by byte-copy sync PR (`coordination-propagate`); it now REFERENCES the versioned
    `FS.GG.Kit` package and MATERIALIZES that kit onto disk from its pin. A hub change reaches this repo
    as an ordinary Renovate bump of the pin (in Directory.Packages.local.props), and `kit-materialize.yml`
    re-runs the materialize on that PR — the same shape lockfile-sync uses for packages.lock.json.

    This project exists ONLY to reference the package and drive its materialize. It compiles nothing and
    is deliberately NOT in the solution, so the normal build never touches it. Materialize explicitly:

        dotnet build .config/kit/FS.GG.Kit.receiver.proj -t:FsggKitMaterialize

    The materialized files (skills, scripts/fsgg-coord, .config/dotnet-tools.json, and — because this is
    a `build-config` receiver — Directory.Build.props / Directory.Packages.props) are COMMITTED: the
    materialize is the write arm that replaces the byte-copy, not a live per-build input, so it does NOT
    run on every build (FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild=false, per the .github#1280 review). The committed bytes
    are content-addressed and byte-identical to canonical, so `coordination-coherence` stays green.

    ONE RUNTIME ROOT IS NO LONGER MATERIALIZED INTO (FS-GG/.github#1734, ADR-0067 §9 phase 4, stage 4,
    2026-07-28). This repo used to commit its 21 skills TWICE — 41 tracked files under `.claude/skills`
    and 41 byte-identical ones under `.agents/skills`. It commits them once. `.agents/skills` is a VIEW
    root: still in the runtime contract, still loaded by both runtimes, but git-ignored and RESOLVED
    from `.claude/skills` at checkout instead of being a second committed copy of the same bytes. See
    the FsggKitViewSkillRoots block below and FsggGameGenerateSkillView at the bottom.
    `coordination-coherence` narrows with it — it derives its roots from FsggKitSkillRoots — and stays
    green with no gate edit anywhere.

    THIS REPO'S OWN 17 SKILLS WERE IN BOTH ROOTS AND STAY VISIBLE IN BOTH. `.claude/skills` and
    `.agents/skills` each held the same 21 directories: the kit's 4 (`check-board`,
    `cross-repo-coordination`, `intra-repo-parallel-work`, `pnext-item`) plus `cut-general-release` and
    16 `fs-gg-sdd-*` skills this repo owns. `diff -r` between the two roots was SILENT before this
    change, so nothing lived only in the retired copy and nothing had to be relocated first — the view
    reproduces all 21. That check is not optional: on FS.GG.SDD the same diff found a tracked,
    producer-authoritative file with no home under a view (FS.GG.SDD#771), which is why the retirement
    order requires it before any receiver is retired.

    `.codex/skills` IS NOT TOUCHED BY THIS CHANGE. It is the RETIRED root (ADR-0067 §5,
    FS-GG/.github#1636); FS.GG.Kit's retired-root sweep already withdrew the kit-owned files from it,
    and the 17 skills remaining there (`cut-general-release` + 16 `fs-gg-sdd-*`) are this repo's OWN.
    ADR-0065 §Retiring a root forbids anyone hand-deleting them, and this change does not.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A TFM is required by the SDK; nothing is compiled. Matches the repo's band (global.json). -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Tooling-only: no source, no output, no lockfile of its own (the gate locks the solution, not this). -->
    <EnableDefaultItems>false</EnableDefaultItems>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>false</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

    <!-- ADR-0067 §9 phase 4, stage 4 (FS-GG/.github#1734). `.agents/skills` is a VIEW root now, not a
         materialized one: still in the runtime contract, still loaded by both runtimes, but its content
         is RESOLVED from `.claude/skills` at checkout by `scripts/skill-view generate` instead of being
         a second committed copy. THE RUNTIME ROOT SET IS UNCHANGED — ADR-0065 §A root's three
         dispositions makes it the UNION of these two properties, and that union is still ADR-0011's
         two roots. Nothing is retired here; one copy stopped existing.

         WHY `.claude/skills` IS THE LIVE ROOT AND NOT THE VIEW. The kit's four skill rows source
         `.claude/skills/<id>` in FS-GG/.github `registry/repos.yml`, and `registry/repos.lock`
         content-addresses those exact paths — so the materialize writes there, and
         `coordination-coherence` grades those bytes against this repo's pin. Making `.agents/skills`
         the live root instead would rename a `kit:` source, obliging a kit republish and a
         seven-receiver fan-out for a path rename. See FS-GG/.github
         `docs/coordination/skill-apparatus-retirement-order.md` §3.

         BOTH PROPERTIES ARE DECLARED EXPLICITLY, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
         `scripts/check-skill-view-roots.sh` grades their union on this repo's required
         `Build-config drift check (shared-build-config)` context and fails CLOSED on an absent
         property — because an undeclared property would silently evaluate to the package default and
         hide exactly the regression that alarm exists for. -->
    <FsggKitSkillRoots>.claude/skills</FsggKitSkillRoots>
    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>.agents/skills</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>

    <!-- Materialize into the repo ROOT (two levels up from .config/kit/), on demand — never on build. -->
    <FsggKitReceiverRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../'))</FsggKitReceiverRoot>
    <FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>false</FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>

    <!-- This repo `receives: build-config`, so it also materializes Directory.Build.props /
         Directory.Packages.props to the repo root. First adoption of a hand-authored .props needs
         FsggKitAdoptBuildConfig=true for one run (.github#387); the canonical files here already carry
         the 'Source of truth: FS-GG/.github' marker, so no adopt is needed and the overwrite is free. -->
    <FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig>true</FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Version pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.local.props (Renovate-managed), like every other pin. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FS.GG.Kit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE VIEW IS GENERATED BEFORE IT IS ASSERTED, IN EVERY TREE THE MATERIALIZE RUNS IN.

    `FsggKitCheckSkillView` (ADR-0067 §8, AfterTargets=FsggKitMaterialize) fails the build when a view
    root is absent, dangling, or partly visible — deliberately, because all three of those are exit 0
    with no diagnostic in BOTH runtimes. A view root is NOT committed (a committed symlink degrades to
    a plain text file under `git -c core.symlinks=false`, ADR-0067 §6), so EVERY fresh checkout starts
    without one: a clean clone, a new worktree, `kit-materialize.yml`'s runner, and — the one that
    bites THIS repo specifically — gate.yml's `build-config-drift` job, which is a REQUIRED context
    and runs `-t:FsggKitMaterialize` on a bare checkout on every PR. Measured on a fresh clone at the
    retirement commit: without this target the materialize FAILS with "view skill root '.agents/skills'
    is ABSENT or a DANGLING link", so the required build-config gate would red on a tree nobody
    touched.

    So the generate belongs next to the assertion, not in a workflow step that only some of those trees
    run. The tool is `scripts/skill-view` — THIS repo's copy, delivered and byte-verified by the
    FS.GG.Kit version this repo pins (a `kind: client` kit row since 0.15.0), never one fetched from a
    moving hub `main`: a receiver's verdict must be a function of the receiver (FS-GG/.github#1584).

    The `roots` flag names the view roots EXPLICITLY rather than letting the tool fall back to
    ADR-0011's default pair. The fallback would also try to resolve `.claude/skills`, this repo's
    TRACKED live root, and `skill-view` refuses to generate over a tracked root with no override, so it
    would be a refusal rather than a no-op.

    The `Condition` guards on the PROPERTY, so if a later change empties FsggKitViewSkillRoots this
    target stops running with it instead of generating a view nothing asserts. That same emptying is
    what `scripts/check-skill-view-roots.sh` reds on.
  -->
  <Target Name="FsggGameGenerateSkillView" BeforeTargets="FsggKitCheckSkillView"
          Condition="'$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots)' != ''">
    <Exec WorkingDirectory="$(FsggKitReceiverRoot)"
          Command="bash scripts/skill-view generate --source $(FsggKitSkillRoots) --roots &quot;$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots.Replace(';',' '))&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
